\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Reflection and the signs of the slab operator}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let #{r(x,l)=(x,L+1-l)} and let #{R} pull fields back along
#{r}. Reflect every transverse link to the corresponding layer,
and put #{U_s^r(x,l)=U_s(x,L-l)^{-1}} on the normal steps.
For the \vocabk{slab operator}{fdip-01AE} the unitary
#{J=\Gamma E_sR} satisfies
##{JH_L(U)J^{-1}=-H_L(U^r).}
If #{U^r} is a gauge transform of #{U} by fiber unitaries
commuting with #{\Gamma}, then the positive and negative parts
of #{H_L(U)} have equal dimensions. There are invertible slab
operators for scalar links in dimension #{2} whose positive
and negative dimensions differ.}

\proof{
\p{Pullback preserves the transverse forward steps with
reflected links and exchanges the forward normal step with the
reflected backward step. The product #{\Gamma E_s} commutes
with transverse directions and link transports, and exchanges
#{P_-} with #{P_+}. Together the two operations take
#{K_{L,U}} to #{K_{L,U^r}}. The same product negates #{\Gamma}
under conjugation, giving the displayed sign. If #{U^r=U^g},
the site action #{\mathcal G} of #{g} conjugates #{H_L(U)}
to #{H_L(U^r)}. Hence #{\mathcal G^{-1}J} exchanges
the positive and negative parts of #{H_L(U)} isometrically.}

\p{Take a transverse circle of period one, #{L=2}, #{F=\mathbb C}
and spin dimension #{2}. Let #{E_t,E_s}
be the transverse and normal Clifford directions, and choose
#{\Gamma=-\ui E_sE_t}, so #{\ui\Gamma E_t=E_s}.
Use the identity normal link. Take the transverse shifts on
the bottom and top loops to be #{\ue^{\ui\theta}} and
#{\ue^{-\ui\theta}} with #{0<\theta<\pi /2}. At
#{aM=2-\cos\theta} the on-site scalar is zero. Put
#{b=\sin\theta}. The on-site contributions to #{aH_L(U)}
are #{b E_s} on the bottom and #{-b E_s} on the top.
On the bottom #{\im P_-} and top #{\im P_+} both therefore
act by #{-b}, and the normal hops vanish there.}

\p{On the orthogonal complement, bottom #{\im P_+} and top
#{\im P_-}, one has #{aH_L(U)=b\bbI -Q} where
#{Q=\Gamma P_-V_U+\Gamma P_+V_U^\dagger}. This self-adjoint
map exchanges the two lines unitarily and squares to the
identity. Giving opposite signs to the layers exchanges the sign spaces of #{Q}. Since #{0<b<1}, #{b\bbI -Q} has one positive and
one negative dimension. Counting the two exposed lines gives
#{\nu_+(H_L(U))=1} and #{\nu_-(H_L(U))=3}.}

\p{The inequality persists on an even transverse circle of period
#{4}. Use the same layerwise shifts times the free transverse
translation with #{\cos\theta=12/13}. The constant character keeps its imbalance. On the other
characters the free operator at #{aM=1} has lower bound #{1/a}:
at #{p=\pm\pi /2} use the square in \ref{fdip-01AH}, and
at #{p=\pi} use #{d=2} and #{\|N\|\le1}. Its positive and
negative dimensions coincide by reflection on each character.
The two shift coefficients change by conjugate factors on the
orthogonal ranges of #{P_{t,-},P_{t,+}}, giving a norm change
in #{aK_{L,U}} of at most #{|1-\ue^{\ui\theta}|}. Including
the mass change bounds the perturbation of #{aH_L} by
#{\sqrt{2/13}+1/13<1}. The form therefore stays strictly
positive and strictly negative on the respective free sign
spaces. Each projects injectively to the corresponding new
sign space. Their dimensions sum to the whole dimension, so both persist.}
}
